#{[17592186496263 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496263 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496263 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336301" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496263 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496263 #edb/ref 1071 "The gravitational force between two objects, of masses $(8.2\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(5.6\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg},$ has a magnitude of $(2.0\\times {10}^{-6})\\,\\text{N}.$\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496263 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5f6f1f55-e6a2-5e50-9cd8-7d4ea9cc100b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496263 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(8.2\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(5.6\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(8.2\\times {10}^{5})\\cdot (5.6\\times {10}^{6})}{2.0\\times {10}^{-6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 8.2\\cdot 5.6}{2.0}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{5}\\cdot {10}^{6}}{{10}^{-6}} \\\\  & =153.1432\\times {10}^{6} \\\\  & =1.531432\\times {10}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{1.531432\\times {10}^{8}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1.531432}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{8}} \\\\  & =(1.2375…)\\times {10}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the distance between the two objects is\n$(\\,1.2\\times {10}^{4}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496317 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496317 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496317 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336289" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496317 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496317 #edb/ref 1071 "The centers of two objects are separated by a distance of $(3.0\\times {10}^{3})\\,\\text{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(4.0\\times {10}^{-6})\\,\\text{N}.$ If one object has mass $(2.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of the other object? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe mass of the other object is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{kg}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496317 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e3990de5-dc6f-5bc7-bcf1-3490082ecdc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496317 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{2}$ be the unknown mass.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make ${m}_{2}$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(4.0\\times {10}^{-6})\\cdot (3.0\\times {10}^{3})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (2.5\\times {10}^{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4.0\\cdot (3.0)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 2.5}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-6}\\cdot ({10}^{3})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{5}} \\\\  & =(2.158…)\\times {10}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the mass of the other object is\n$(\\,2.2\\times {10}^{6}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496326 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496326 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496326 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336279" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496326 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496326 #edb/ref 1071 "The Sun has a mass of $(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Jupiter has a mass of $(1.90\\times {10}^{27})\\,\\text{kg}.$\nThe average distance between the centers of the Sun and Jupiter is $(7.78\\times {10}^{11})\\,\\text{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the Sun and Jupiter? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe magnitude of the force between the Sun and Jupiter is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{N}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496326 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e90c4b66-398a-540c-92b6-fce26b283a8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496326 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Jupiter. Then, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\cdot (1.90\\times {10}^{27})}{(7.78\\times {10}^{11})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 1.99\\cdot 1.90}{(7.78)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}\\cdot {10}^{27}}{({10}^{11})^{2}} \\\\  & =(0.416\\,651…)\\times {10}^{24} \\\\  & =(4.166\\,51…)\\times {10}^{23}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the gravitational force between the Sun and Jupiter is\n$(\\,4.17\\times {10}^{23}\\,)\\,\\text{N}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
